WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.560
Really interesting point in my career where I had finished my undergraduate studies.

00:04.560 --> 00:08.120
I was getting really into language design and compilers.

00:08.120 --> 00:13.720
My now husband at the time, and he's like elbowing me in the side, like, you should do it, you should do it.

00:13.720 --> 00:23.480
Many people still say this to me, like I gave some of these talks about my journey in core development years ago.

00:23.480 --> 00:26.500
Hi, everyone, and welcome to another episode of Behind the Commit.

00:26.500 --> 00:38.200
Have you ever wondered what it takes to go from picking up Python as a college hobby to being personally mentored by Guidovan Rossum and ultimately implementing one of the most controversial features in Python history?

00:38.200 --> 00:40.680
In this episode, I had the chance of Emily Morehouse.

00:40.680 --> 00:49.440
Emily is a Cpython core developer, a former member of the Python Steering Council, ESF fellow, and the co-founder and director of Engineering at Apple Soft Development Agency.

00:49.440 --> 00:57.960
She holds three degrees from Florida State University in computer science, theater, and criminology, and got started in Python just because it was more fun than C++.

00:57.960 --> 01:04.840
If someone had told you 10 or 15 years ago that you would become a core developer, how would you react?

01:04.840 --> 01:07.240
I would be shocked.

01:07.240 --> 01:15.960
Yeah, I don't think that I would have even thought that that was a possibility or really even like known what that would have meant at the time.

01:15.960 --> 01:24.140
I actually got started programming in college, and my first courses that I took were actually in C++.

01:24.140 --> 01:26.360
Like that's what the university curriculum was taught in.

01:26.360 --> 01:34.200
And I sort of picked up Python just as a hobby and something that was much easier to build cool things with than, you know, compared to C++.

01:34.200 --> 01:46.280
And I don't think that I even like registered at the time that there was a group of people developing that language that I would ultimately fall in love with and could participate in.

01:46.280 --> 01:47.360
What did you study?

01:47.360 --> 01:48.340
Interesting question.

01:48.340 --> 01:50.540
I actually have three degrees.

01:50.540 --> 01:54.480
So I studied theater, computer science, and criminology.

01:54.480 --> 01:56.780
And what was your journey into core development?

01:56.780 --> 02:00.780
And it's kind of, it is kind of a long story, so I'll try to keep it short.

02:00.780 --> 02:08.400
But I had been attending like a couple of Python conferences in like 2014 or so.

02:08.400 --> 02:13.300
And I was at this really interesting point in my career where I had finished my undergraduate studies.

02:13.300 --> 02:27.620
I was getting really into language design and compilers and was kind of at this fork in the road of going to grad school or moving to Silicon Valley and like taking a job or starting a company.

02:27.620 --> 02:33.800
And I wound up dropping out of grad school, the little bit that I did, and started this company.

02:33.800 --> 02:37.160
We really like got involved in the Python community.

02:37.160 --> 02:39.100
It became like a really big driving force for us.

02:39.100 --> 02:48.940
And so through that involvement, it was actually at one of those conferences that Guido, during a keynote, said that he wanted more women core developers.

02:48.940 --> 02:51.300
They didn't have any female core developers at that time.

02:51.300 --> 02:54.180
He offered to personally mentor anyone who was interested.

02:54.180 --> 02:58.300
And it was around 2016 or 17, something like that, right?

02:58.300 --> 03:06.440
Yeah, I think it was like 2015 that he first made the announcement and then 2016 that he hadn't seen anybody come forth.

03:06.440 --> 03:08.580
And that's when he offered to like personally mentor people.

03:08.580 --> 03:16.420
So I just remember looking at my now husband at the time and he's like elbowing me in the side like, you should do it, you should do it.

03:16.420 --> 03:19.540
And I'm like deer in the headlights, like terrified.

03:19.540 --> 03:20.840
What was your first thought?

03:20.840 --> 03:22.480
What were you afraid of?

03:22.480 --> 03:31.220
I think that especially then Guido is very much a figurehead in the community and a little bit like godlike, right?

03:31.220 --> 03:35.380
Like this person that created this language and brought us all together.

03:35.380 --> 03:42.100
It was just very intimidating to talk to him, especially being like as early in my career as I was.

03:42.100 --> 03:51.140
I just felt like I really looked up to him and was a bit intimidated in that sense that I didn't really know what it was that I could offer to somebody like that.

03:51.140 --> 03:51.400
Right.

03:51.400 --> 03:56.760
So he made an announcement and said that we are searching for more core developers.

03:56.760 --> 03:58.540
And what did you do?

03:58.540 --> 03:59.520
I emailed him.

03:59.520 --> 04:00.800
I took him up on it.

04:00.800 --> 04:06.260
I think one of the first things he did was invited me to the language summit that they have at PyCon US.

04:06.260 --> 04:16.740
I think I was one of the first folks that, you know, was in that room and wasn't necessarily a core developer and just kind of like dove in headfirst.

04:16.740 --> 04:17.240
And then he did.

04:17.240 --> 04:18.720
I mean, he did start mentoring me.

04:18.720 --> 04:22.420
I would meet with him every few weeks and we would just kind of like work on different problems.

04:22.420 --> 04:27.940
And he would kind of help me find the right thing to work on that really got my attention.

04:27.940 --> 04:33.160
And I think that's the hardest thing about getting started is like finding where to start and what's going to interest you.

04:33.160 --> 04:34.260
So. So.

04:34.260 --> 04:38.460
And how did you feel for the first time when you went there and you saw lots of core developers?

04:38.460 --> 04:41.560
Did you feel like I do belong here?

04:41.560 --> 04:42.960
What do I do here?

04:42.960 --> 04:45.080
I don't know anything.

04:45.080 --> 04:45.460
Yeah.

04:45.460 --> 04:46.900
I mean, I think that there's a part of that.

04:46.900 --> 04:56.320
I think that it becomes a little bit less intimidating when you realize how much you can understand or how much you can learn quickly and how much people are willing to explain things to you.

04:56.320 --> 04:59.140
I do also remember just feeling like really cool.

04:59.140 --> 05:04.800
Like I got to be in the room and I think that was just like an incredible feeling.

05:04.800 --> 05:08.560
And how did you feel being mentored by Guido personally?

05:08.560 --> 05:14.300
Yeah, I think that it really kind of stripped away the pretense, right?

05:14.300 --> 05:22.540
Like the intimidation and all of that stuff because he was so welcoming and like was always the first one to say he didn't know something.

05:22.540 --> 05:25.480
And would really just be like, hey, let's figure it out together.

05:25.480 --> 05:28.620
Like, I don't remember how this old piece of code works in the parser.

05:28.620 --> 05:30.320
Like, let's sit down and walk through it together.

05:30.320 --> 05:32.600
And I was like, oh, man, like he doesn't know everything.

05:32.600 --> 05:42.280
Like I can learn from his processes and he can like help work through it with me instead of just feeling like he had to, you know, spoon feed everything.

05:42.280 --> 05:43.800
And how did the onboarding look like?

05:43.800 --> 05:46.880
Was it done async remotely or you had it in person?

05:46.880 --> 05:49.320
Yeah, it was all async.

05:49.320 --> 05:52.680
I think this was back when he was working at Dropbox.

05:52.680 --> 05:58.540
So I remember we would just do, you know, weekly or biweekly video calls just on Fridays.

05:58.540 --> 06:05.300
And that kind of became my little like open source ritual to a certain extent, whether I was working with Guido or I was working on things on my own.

06:05.300 --> 06:09.440
Or I had one other mentor named Eric Snow who really helped me out a lot in the beginning.

06:09.440 --> 06:16.120
And that was kind of like my sacred open source time where I'd go book a conference room back when we were actually in offices.

06:16.120 --> 06:18.560
Kind of hunker down.

06:18.560 --> 06:19.080
Yeah.

06:19.080 --> 06:22.160
And what were some of the first features you worked on?

06:22.160 --> 06:25.980
Did you start with bad fixes, documentation or features?

06:25.980 --> 06:28.220
Like what is a good way how to get started?

06:28.220 --> 06:28.540
Yeah.

06:28.540 --> 06:32.740
So I remember I picked up a couple of bug fixes.

06:32.740 --> 06:36.540
But I think that this is one of, again, it's so hard to find where to get started.

06:36.540 --> 06:37.440
Because there are so many possibilities.

06:37.440 --> 06:39.520
So many possibilities.

06:39.520 --> 06:44.780
And also the language is very bold, but also very well known.

06:44.780 --> 06:52.720
So a lot of the bugs that you'd find are either very easy bugs that, you know, quickly get fixed by somebody who's just like available and ready.

06:52.720 --> 07:00.180
And so when you're a newcomer and you start going through like the backlog of tickets, you're like, oh, man, like there's a reason why somebody else didn't pick up this ticket.

07:00.180 --> 07:02.460
And now you have this like puzzle.

07:02.460 --> 07:03.440
Yeah.

07:03.440 --> 07:04.320
I did that at first.

07:04.320 --> 07:10.000
And then the big thing that I was able to do was implement the walrus operator.

07:10.000 --> 07:12.160
You can edit that one.

07:12.160 --> 07:15.020
It is a very controversial feature, right?

07:15.020 --> 07:16.320
It is very controversial.

07:16.320 --> 07:20.800
The pep itself really just like broke a lot of things in the community, broke a lot of things for Guido.

07:20.800 --> 07:26.060
And that was ultimately one of the things that caused him to step down as BDFL.

07:26.060 --> 07:33.180
But for me, it was just like the perfect thing where I was really looking for something that had a lot more depth to it to really sink my teeth in.

07:33.180 --> 07:36.240
I was obviously very interested in like the language design side of it.

07:36.240 --> 07:45.620
So getting in there and messing with the tokenizer and the parser and adding new syntax to the language was just like the absolute perfect fit for me and was probably the most exciting thing that I've done in Python.

07:45.620 --> 07:49.660
Did you also feel supported by other core developers?

07:49.660 --> 07:52.480
Because you mentioned that you were working a lot with Guido.

07:52.480 --> 07:57.160
Are there any other people that you work with or you mostly work with Guido only?

07:57.160 --> 08:01.240
Yeah, I think that Guido worked with me directly on that work.

08:01.240 --> 08:04.120
I always felt very supported by other core developers.

08:04.120 --> 08:05.100
They were always very welcoming.

08:05.100 --> 08:12.060
Actually, for a certain period of time, Eric Snow and I would have these sort of like virtual working sessions like pre-pandemic.

08:12.060 --> 08:16.540
So this was kind of like an interesting thing that probably most people weren't thinking about doing.

08:16.540 --> 08:23.260
But yeah, he and I would like jump on a call and kind of use each other to walk through problems when we had it or kind of like rubber duck.

08:23.260 --> 08:28.220
So he was definitely a person that was like there with me through all of that.

08:28.220 --> 08:32.260
And how can you inspire more people, especially women, to get into core development?

08:32.260 --> 08:34.500
Lots of people feel very overwhelmed.

08:34.500 --> 08:39.140
They feel like there are no low-hanging fruits because it's a language that's been around for many years.

08:39.140 --> 08:44.680
They also feel like all those people working on the core language, they all have to be experts in the field.

08:44.680 --> 08:46.500
They have to know everything.

08:46.500 --> 08:48.300
But that's not the case.

08:48.300 --> 08:55.820
As you said, how can we inspire more people and to show them that actually it's not as bad as they think it is?

08:55.820 --> 08:56.680
Yeah, absolutely.

08:56.680 --> 09:00.760
I mean, I think that doing more like working in public is always really great.

09:00.760 --> 09:10.120
And the more people who speak about their experiences and how they got started, I think that there's also a lot of people who really want to help people.

09:10.120 --> 09:17.200
So going to conferences and getting involved in the sprints and like meeting people, I think just brings so much more color to it, right?

09:17.200 --> 09:27.700
Like when you have actually met somebody in person and can then associate a person with a GitHub username or something, you kind of know who you can reach out to and look to for support.

09:27.700 --> 09:37.960
And have you ever got feedback from anyone that they, for example, find your work very inspiring or that they saw something and they decided also to start contributing?

09:37.960 --> 09:39.100
Yeah, actually.

09:39.100 --> 09:46.280
I mean, you certainly sent me that's like very sweet email a couple of months ago that was very touching.

09:46.280 --> 09:52.660
But yeah, I'm actually, I'm very surprised at how many people still say this to me.

09:52.660 --> 09:57.560
Like I gave some of these talks about my journey in core development years ago, right?

09:57.560 --> 10:03.860
And I'm always a bit taken aback that it mattered and had that much of an impact.

10:03.860 --> 10:11.620
And except of the core development, you were also the chair of PyCon US for two years and you're also a member of the steering council.

10:11.620 --> 10:13.960
How have those influenced your career?

10:13.960 --> 10:16.140
Yeah, definitely.

10:16.140 --> 10:24.360
Actually, I wound up chairing PyCon US for three years because I was the chair when the pandemic hit and we had to move everything online.

10:24.360 --> 10:31.520
So we did two years of online and then I stuck around for a third year to be able to like actually see it come to fruition in person.

10:31.520 --> 10:36.200
But I think that for me, I don't know that it's had as direct an impact on my career.

10:36.200 --> 10:42.980
I've been running the same company that I have been since I dropped out of college to start it.

10:42.980 --> 10:56.600
But I think that it's really given me the right perspective to be confident in my skills and to figure things out and to have a strong leadership presence and to really just sort of like lean into that as a path that I could take.

10:56.600 --> 11:04.700
And then certainly the network of people and just the friends that I've made along the way, I think, have been the biggest.

11:04.700 --> 11:08.120
You know, there's some skills that you develop through your community work.

11:08.120 --> 11:11.460
So you mentioned leadership because you work with a large number of people.

11:11.460 --> 11:17.080
Are there any more other skills or other, for example, surprising things that you learn just by working in the community?

11:17.080 --> 11:22.040
I mean, community leadership and organization is hard.

11:22.040 --> 11:29.700
It takes a lot to get people on board and to keep people invested and motivated and to kind of make sure that you have the right support.

11:29.700 --> 11:35.560
And I see a lot of parallels in leading, you know, client projects in our consultancy.

11:35.560 --> 11:42.740
You're like leading a team and finding ways to really bring people together and collaborate, I think is the biggest thing.

11:42.740 --> 11:45.480
Most people organizing PyCon are volunteers, right?

11:45.480 --> 11:52.100
So that's another challenge to keep people motivated when there is no monetary incentives.

11:52.100 --> 11:53.080
Absolutely.

11:53.080 --> 11:58.680
But I mean, I think that's the biggest driving force behind Python is its community and how invested people are.

11:58.680 --> 12:03.420
And what motivated you to chair PyCon?

12:03.420 --> 12:06.740
Or to run for the steering council, for example?

12:06.740 --> 12:07.320
Yeah.

12:07.320 --> 12:11.620
So I actually had not thought about chairing PyCon US.

12:11.620 --> 12:12.900
I was invited to.

12:12.900 --> 12:23.560
And I remember having the conversation with E, who was the previous conference chair before me, being like, man, I don't know what my life is going to look like in like four or five years.

12:23.560 --> 12:28.760
Because I was I chaired a co-chaired for a couple of years and then ultimately chaired in.

12:28.760 --> 12:33.140
It's funny looking back on that conversation, because how could I have known what was coming?

12:33.140 --> 12:33.260
Right.

12:33.260 --> 12:34.500
Like we had the pandemic.

12:34.500 --> 12:35.700
I had a kid.

12:35.700 --> 12:36.220
No one.

12:36.220 --> 12:38.440
Like I had no idea.

12:38.440 --> 12:47.000
But I think that my biggest driving force behind doing it was knowing how impactful it had been on my career and how important that community was to me.

12:47.000 --> 12:53.160
Being able to continue to be the voice of that community and bringing people together was an honor.

12:53.160 --> 12:55.340
That's nice to hear.

12:55.340 --> 12:57.080
So you mentioned that you have a kid.

12:57.080 --> 13:04.640
How has parenthood impacted your career or the way you think about the open source community or work in general?

13:04.640 --> 13:16.400
Yeah, it has definitely like significantly cut down on free time and has also really just kind of reprioritized the way that I want to spend my time.

13:16.400 --> 13:25.400
I would much, much rather spend my weekends and my evenings actually hanging out with my daughter and engaging with her and spending that time while she's young.

13:25.400 --> 13:27.000
And I know that it's fleeting.

13:27.000 --> 13:33.500
And I know that one day I'll have more evenings and weekends to come back to the things that used to fill my time.

13:33.500 --> 13:40.400
But I think it also has been just really cool to have all these like friends over the years where we now come to these conferences.

13:40.400 --> 13:54.760
We catch up and either I show pictures of my kids to them or it's really cool to be at EuroPython where they actually have child care and I could bring her really easily and get people who I have known and loved for so long to actually like meet my daughter in person.

13:54.760 --> 13:56.560
It's just super cool.

13:56.560 --> 13:59.580
And how to get involved with core development.

13:59.580 --> 14:04.900
So I would like to contribute either to documentation or to the code base, for example.

14:04.900 --> 14:08.900
What are the best first steps?

14:08.900 --> 14:09.380
Yeah.

14:09.380 --> 14:13.060
So everyone sort of points you to like the dev guide online.

14:13.060 --> 14:15.400
There's a ton of resources and how to get started.

14:15.400 --> 14:21.880
Whether it's editing documentation or, you know, getting CPython built and running locally on your machine.

14:21.880 --> 14:33.320
But I would really recommend if you have the opportunity to meet people in person, go to an event and just tell people what you're interested in and find folks that are working in that area.

14:33.320 --> 14:36.040
And tell them that you're looking for a mentor.

14:36.040 --> 14:41.820
And that's really like the best first step is to have somebody that can kind of take you under their wing and lead you through the process.

14:41.820 --> 14:48.780
Ultimately, that's like you need to have somebody who's sort of like mentoring you and sponsoring you to become a core developer anyways.

14:48.780 --> 14:54.900
And it's just nice to have that person to kind of depend on and keep you, you know, keep you on the right path.

14:54.900 --> 14:56.140
Are you mentoring anyone?

14:56.140 --> 14:57.840
Can people contact you?

14:57.840 --> 15:00.920
I am not mentoring anyone currently.

15:00.920 --> 15:03.520
Definitely strapped on time these days.

15:03.520 --> 15:05.320
I would love to do that one day in the future.

15:05.320 --> 15:10.120
But I know that there are tons of folks there is like a Discord channel.

15:10.120 --> 15:10.880
There's discourse.

15:10.880 --> 15:14.880
There's, you know, tons of different ways to reach people.

15:14.880 --> 15:15.440
Okay.

15:15.440 --> 15:16.560
Thank you for coming.

15:16.560 --> 15:17.500
Thanks for having me.
